// The remote management session (component D21): how an owner on another
// machine inspects, steers and updates a Silica machine.

/// A remote management session: the machine's own protocols, called from
/// another machine.
///
/// Provided by the remote management service (`remote`) on TCP port 22 to
/// an owner whose public key the machine was given. A session is a Noise
/// IK handshake (`Noise_IK_25519_ChaChaPoly_BLAKE2s`, prologue
/// `silica.remote 2`), then messages each sealed with the session's keys.
/// Every message on the TCP connection is its length (2 bytes, big-endian)
/// and that many bytes. The first is the owner's handshake message, whose
/// payload is when it was made (the owner's clock in nanoseconds since
/// 1970, 8 bytes, big-endian), the second the machine's answer (no
/// payload); a machine that does not know the owner's key, cannot open the
/// message, or has taken a first message from that owner made as late or
/// later (one recorded and sent again) closes the connection without
/// answering. A connection is an owner's session once its first sealed
/// message opens; the machine closes one that has not proved so within
/// 10 s, and a session no whole message arrives on for an hour (or what
/// the machine's owner set). After them, each sealed message's
/// plaintext is one call or one answer:
///
/// - a call: a channel (1 byte), then an IDL request (`spec/IDL.md`,
///   header and payload, at most 4000 bytes, carrying no capability);
/// - its answer: the same channel, a status (1 byte: 0 the IDL reply
///   follows; 1 the protocol's provider went away during the call,
///   `peer_gone`; 2 the machine holds no capability to it, `revoked`;
///   3 no such channel), then the IDL reply.
///
/// The owner sends one call and waits for its answer before the next.
/// Channel 0 is this protocol; channel n is the n-th name `protocols`
/// answers (from 1): `silica.system.Manager` and `silica.log.Reader`, so
/// that what the shell does at the console a session does from afar
/// (`silica-remote update` sends a program with the manager's `stage` and
/// `update`, one piece a call). A call that needs a capability cannot be
/// made from another machine (`add_log`): its provider sees the capability
/// missing and refuses it.
/// `silica_remote` (system/remote/proto) implements the framing, and the
/// host's `silica-remote` is a client written on it.
protocol silica.remote.Session version 1 {
    /// The protocols this session carries, `names[i]` on channel `i + 1`.
    protocols @1 () -> (names: array<string>);
}
